me under a lot of  criticism for the slow  moving investigation many human rights  groups really have criticized it for  lacking credibility and lacking  transparency  and zeno we saw in the aftermath of that  explosion but also  in the run-up to it as well furious  demonstrations in the streets of beirut  particularly over allegations of  corruption amongst the government  is there any indication although as we  know this is a breaking story but is  there any early indication that this  might go some way  to try to allay the anger that the  people have felt about this  yes a lot of public anger been speaking  uh  to people they're saying well yes this  is a step forward  but it's definitely not enough and  there's another thing  people have lost faith in the  authorities even in the judiciary which  many have accused of being  politicized they really believe that  this could be part of a game some will  say why are you pointing the finger at  dieb he took office just  a few months uh before the the the  explosion a little-known figure who's  not backed by any political party who  had no influence at the port  and that they should go after quote  unquote but people are calling the big  players the different political parties  all of them had representatives at that  port all of them had influence at that  point  and that port you know there has been a  lot of corruption allegations  but there are others who believe that  jeb didn't do his job  because in july security officials  put a report on his table on his table  and on the president's table  saying that there's thousands of tons of  ammonium nitrate that they report and  they pose a security risk  and neither him nor the president did  anything about it in fact he was even  scheduled died was scheduled to visit  the port before that explosion to visit  that warehouse  and he did it and he acknowledged that  and he said that he postponed the visit  because  um he was receiving conflicting reports  uh so some wi